The acceptance test asserts that `tests/test_other_module.py::TestKid::test_from_base` is a *valid* probe, but in the repo this test builds, that node names no existing test: the only file written is `tests/test_other_module.py`, whose whole content is `from base import Base

def test_fn():
    pass

class TestKid(Base):
    pass
` — i.e. `TestKid` is empty and nothing anywhere in the test defines `test_from_base` (no `base` module is created). So a validator that accepts this probe cannot have verified from the probed file that the named test exists; whatever rule admits it (e.g. "class declares a base clause ⇒ accept any member name", or "the file has an import we cannot resolve ⇒ accept") admits equally `tests/test_other_module.py::TestKid::test_totally_made_up`. Running `ddflow review triage <id> --confirmed --probe tests/test_other_module.py::TestKid::test_totally_made_up` on that fixture therefore records the triage and passes the gate, with a node pytest cannot collect (collection of that file raises on `from base import Base`), which is exactly bug Bb049f0ae06 ("a non-existent test node attached to a confirmed finding and ddflow recorded it and passed the gate"). Compare the sibling refusal case at line 28 (`tests/test_real.py::TestB::test_real`, a fabricated member of a base-less class) which is correctly refused — the new tests cover the base-less case only as a refusal and the based-class case only as an acceptance, so the unsound half of the rule is enshrined by the test rather than caught by it.

`test_a_probe_naming_an_existing_test_or_plain_command_is_accepted` builds a repo containing exactly two test files, `tests/test_real.py` and `tests/test_other_module.py`, and the latter is `from base import Base

def test_fn():
    pass

class TestKid(Base):
    pass
`. `test_from_base` is defined nowhere in the repo, and the base it would come from (`base`) does not exist either — pytest would not even collect this module (`ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'base'`). Yet the test asserts that `tests/test_other_module.py::TestKid::test_from_base` is accepted. The only thing that distinguishes this node from the rejected `tests/test_real.py::TestB::test_real` in the sibling test is that `TestKid` has a parenthesised base class, so the check evidently skips member verification for any class that derives from something. Given input `tests/test_real.py` containing `class TestA(unittest.TestCase): pass`, the probe `tests/test_real.py::TestA::test_does_not_exist` therefore still passes unverified — exactly the class of input (a probe naming a test node that does not exist) that Bb049f0ae06 and the previous confirmed finding #1 were about. The fix verifies member membership only for classes without a base, and the acceptance test encodes that hole as required behaviour; the stated intent ("the probe of a confirmed finding should be verified to name an existing test or command") is not met for derived classes.

